::GEO_U04_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 6 and 8. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 10#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>: 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 100, so c = 10.
~B. 14#Adding the legs gives 14, but the Pythagorean Theorem uses squares of side lengths.
~C. &radic;14#The values must be squared before adding, so &radic;14 does not match the theorem.
~D. 48#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation fits a right triangle with legs 5 and 12 and hypotenuse c? {
~A. 5 + 12 = c<sup>2</sup>#The Pythagorean Theorem requires squaring both legs, not adding them directly.
=B. 5<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>#The legs are squared and added to equal the hypotenuse squared.
~C. 5<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> = 12<sup>2</sup>#This treats 12 as the hypotenuse, but c is identified as the hypotenuse.
~D. 5(12) = c<sup>2</sup>#The theorem uses a sum of squares, not a product of legs.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has hypotenuse 13 and one leg 5. What is the other leg? {
~A. 8#Subtracting 5 from 13 ignores the squares in the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. &radic;194#Adding the squares would make a larger hypotenuse, but the hypotenuse is already 13.
=C. 12#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 13<sup>2</sup> - 5<sup>2</sup> = 169 - 25 = 144, so b = 12.
~D. 18#Adding the known side lengths does not find a missing leg.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which side is always opposite the right angle in a right triangle? {
~A. A leg#A leg forms the right angle; it is not opposite the right angle.
~B. The shorter side#The shorter side may be a leg, but the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse.
~C. The adjacent side#Adjacent depends on a chosen acute angle, not the right angle.
=D. The hypotenuse#The hypotenuse is the longest side and is opposite the right angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which set of side lengths can form a right triangle? {
=A. 9, 12, 15#9<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 81 + 144 = 225 = 15<sup>2</sup>, so the set forms a right triangle.
~B. 4, 5, 6#4<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> = 41, not 6<sup>2</sup>.
~C. 5, 7, 9#5<sup>2</sup> + 7<sup>2</sup> = 74, not 9<sup>2</sup>.
~D. 6, 8, 12#6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 100, not 12<sup>2</sup>.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder, wall, and ground form a right triangle. The ladder is the side opposite the right angle. What is the ladder? {
~A. A leg#The wall and ground are the legs because they form the right angle.
=B. The hypotenuse#The side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse.
~C. The shorter side#The hypotenuse is the longest side, not the shorter side.
~D. The included angle#The ladder is a side length, not an angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What is &radic;49? {
~A. 14#14 is double 7, but square root asks for the number whose square is 49.
~B. 24.5#Dividing by 2 does not find a square root.
=C. 7#7<sup>2</sup> = 49, so &radic;49 = 7.
~D. &radic;7#&radic;49 simplifies completely to 7.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 7 and 24. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 31#Adding the legs gives perimeter information, not the hypotenuse.
~B. 17#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean relationship.
~C. &radic;31#The legs must be squared before adding.
=D. 25#7<sup>2</sup> + 24<sup>2</sup> = 49 + 576 = 625, and &radic;625 = 25.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>, which side does c represent in a right triangle? {
=A. The hypotenuse#In the Pythagorean Theorem, c represents the hypotenuse.
~B. Either leg#The legs are represented by a and b in the standard equation.
~C. The smaller leg#The hypotenuse is the longest side, not the smaller leg.
~D. The right angle#c is a side length, not an angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has sides 8, 15, and 17. Why is it a right triangle? {
~A. 8 + 15 = 17#8 + 15 = 23, and side addition is not the right-triangle test.
=B. 8<sup>2</sup> + 15<sup>2</sup> = 17<sup>2</sup>#The two smaller side squares add to the largest side square, satisfying the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 17 - 15 = 8#17 - 15 = 2, and subtraction is not the Pythagorean test.
~D. 8(15) = 17#Multiplication of two sides does not test for a right triangle.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 6 and 8. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 14#Adding the legs gives 14, but the Pythagorean Theorem uses squares of side lengths.
~B. &radic;14#The values must be squared before adding, so &radic;14 does not match the theorem.
=C. 10#Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>: 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 100, so c = 10.
~D. 48#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation fits a right triangle with legs 5 and 12 and hypotenuse c? {
~A. 5 + 12 = c<sup>2</sup>#The Pythagorean Theorem requires squaring both legs, not adding them directly.
~B. 5<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> = 12<sup>2</sup>#This treats 12 as the hypotenuse, but c is identified as the hypotenuse.
~C. 5(12) = c<sup>2</sup>#The theorem uses a sum of squares, not a product of legs.
=D. 5<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>#The legs are squared and added to equal the hypotenuse squared.
}

